 equivalent to finding $n$ such that there exists an integer $k$ for which:\n\\[ 160n - 1399k = 1. \\]\n\nThis is a linear Diophantine equation, and we can solve it using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm. First, we need to apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 160 and 1399.\n\n1. Compute the gcd using the Euclidean Algorithm:\n   \\[\n   1399 = 160 \\cdot 8 + 119,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   160 = 119 \\cdot 1 + 41,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   119 = 41 \\cdot 2 + 37,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   41 = 37 \\cdot 1 + 4,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   37 = 4 \\cdot 9 + 1,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   4 = 4 \\cdot 1 + 0.\n   \\]\n   The gcd is the last non-zero remainder, which is 1. Since the gcd is 1, 160 and 1399 are coprime, and an inverse exists.\n\n2. Now, we use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express 1 as a linear combination of 160 and 1399. We work backwards from the Euclidean Algorithm steps:\n   \\[\n   1 = 37 - 9 \\cdot 4,\n   \\]\n   substitute $4 = 41 - 37$:\n   \\[\n   1 = 37 - 9 \\cdot (41 - 37) = 10 \\cdot 37 - 9 \\cdot 41,\n   \\]\n   substitute $37 = 119 - 2 \\cdot 41$:\n   \\[\n   1 = 10 \\cdot (119 - 2 \\cdot 41) - 9 \\cdot 41 = 10 \\cdot 119 - 29 \\cdot 41,\n   \\]\n   substitute $41 = 160 - 119$:\n   \\[\n   1 = 10 \\cdot 119 - 29 \\cdot (160 - 119) = 39 \\cdot 119 - 29 \\cdot 160,\n   \\]\n   substitute $119 = 1399 - 8 \\cdot 160$:\n   \\[\n   1 = 39 \\cdot (1399 - 8 \\cdot 160) - 29 \\cdot 160 = 39 \\cdot 1399 - 331 \\cdot 160.\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   -331 \\cdot 160 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399}.\n   \\]\n   So, the coefficient of 160, $-331$, is the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399. To find a positive equivalent, we add 1399 to $-331$:\n   \\[\n   -331 + 1399 = 1068.\n   \\]\n\nThus, the integer $n$ that satisfies the condition is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1068}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1068}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
